{"rowToken":"r_0d374055f8c5c873810463ea2cea","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Whether each observed student behavior in Room 12 is a contact-precautions breach or acceptable use of the room's dedicated blood-pressure cuff.","stageEvaluations":{"s_3257930b504f943c60cad87473ab":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_a69e147066181ffc0ca25590ea1c","e_cae1ce2a5712f9eb4e31d71e74f9"],"basis":"The 0730 Room 12 observation is the sole source of the four graded behaviors: gloves without gown, use of Client A's dedicated cuff, glove removal without hand hygiene, and bare-hand door-handle contact."},"s_826f9f674b09c3066fb100eedbe3":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83"],"basis":"Stage 2's 0945 stethoscope removal from Room 16 and the 1030 standard linen bag involve other staff and other rooms, so they cannot classify the student's Room 12 acts."},"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"basis":"The 1215 debrief lists gownless entry, missed hand hygiene after glove removal, and door-handle contact as the student's identified errors while never listing the dedicated cuff, handing over the three breach rows and the one not-a-breach row."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_e8a10ffa4f592a115b0dbc9b70ac","e_c5500db4f364cf91506e23ca3ad8","e_7b085e14ba4cd4d58e8892692384","e_20194263fda197dffe3ff6789ea6"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_a69e147066181ffc0ca25590ea1c","e_cae1ce2a5712f9eb4e31d71e74f9","e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"verbatimSpan":"the Room 12 entry without a gown, missed hand hygiene after glove removal, door-handle contact"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The matrix asks the learner to sort four Stage 1 student acts into breach versus not-a-breach, but the Stage 3 debrief already names three of them as the errors requiring remediation and silently excludes the dedicated cuff. That enumeration is unnecessary for a Stage 1 classification item and reproduces the keyed split. The Chinese debrief lists the same three errors in the same order, so the exposure is equivalent in both languages."}
{"rowToken":"r_9231a83f08e8c5caaf5984640c0f","verdict":"NO_LEAK_COMPLETE_RECORD","testedDecision":"Whether each 44-hour finding, including eGFR 33 against a baseline of 42, indicates improvement or still requires ongoing monitoring.","stageEvaluations":{"s_a30a537bbb8f3fe6e4b4b8a32d22":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_781093034ee772d9ea619b4562bf","e_5b9f14f71a6159fb169a34b5c1b7"],"basis":"The 8-hour stage anchors the deterioration end of the trend the learner grades: four more watery stools, only 200 mL urine, and potassium 3.1 mEq/L."},"s_f68a272842df792f0140d4bcc0e6":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_e5acd5ec09aa93d1b8947371ccd8","e_128bf2528365a339e484a06ffcf6"],"basis":"The 20-hour values (three stools, 280 mL urine, lactate 1.8 mmol/L, creatinine 1.9 mg/dL) supply the midpoint that makes the Stage 3 stool, urine, and lactate rows readable as directional improvement."},"s_aa494f8db2dded77de01432d3ea2":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_c44703e4a3b3924a6a950a4349c9","e_e715f08470c87a1219aa14380f89"],"basis":"The 44-hour update supplies every graded datum: two formed-to-semiformed stools, WBC 12,400/µL, eGFR 33 mL/min, 50 mL/hr urine, and lactate 1.2 mmol/L."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_a3a50d65be466aabc06f60fc9b34","e_4a2b63ef2163799b4b1a16601f4e","e_97f2779715d1e9bfa9e6a68a7811","e_341be58e66fc39da8553fd7e25b9"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_781093034ee772d9ea619b4562bf","e_e5acd5ec09aa93d1b8947371ccd8","e_128bf2528365a339e484a06ffcf6","e_c44703e4a3b3924a6a950a4349c9","e_e715f08470c87a1219aa14380f89","e_54003feea5f2e64128ac40a090e1"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"This is a trend-synthesis item whose own strategy tells the learner to judge direction rather than absolute values, so the 8-hour and 20-hour stages are deliberately used as the comparison points for stool frequency, urine output, and lactate. No stage announces that eGFR 33 is the row needing continued monitoring; that inference still requires comparing it to the baseline of 42. English and Chinese stage updates carry the same numeric series."}
{"rowToken":"r_329eca1031b82fd7edcc00eb451e","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which response to the mother's request to wheel smear-positive Client D outdoors upholds airborne precautions and correct visitor respiratory protection.","stageEvaluations":{"s_3257930b504f943c60cad87473ab":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_931f2afc1daa0116bf8de24f68d4"],"basis":"Stage 1 covers the bladder scanner between Clients A and B and Client C's ungowned daughter; it never mentions Client D, the AIIR, or the outdoor request."},"s_826f9f674b09c3066fb100eedbe3":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_958f43d0b61ecc8e03a926abfbee"],"basis":"The 0915 request from the unmasked mother plus the 1130 note that the morning AFB smear is still positive (2+) establish the smear-positive status the correct reply depends on."},"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"basis":"The 1300 entry has the nurse deliver the keyed teaching almost verbatim: no non-essential trips while smear-positive, patient surgical mask only for medically necessary transport, and N95 instruction and offering for visitors."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_75ca6782fad0393f2bb1d53c6f41","e_ed8221809303f619c790f5145068","e_c859d42d15620c83d282fa1b92d8","e_e97436dedf506f4ad21e71a4034f"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_931f2afc1daa0116bf8de24f68d4","e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_958f43d0b61ecc8e03a926abfbee","e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"verbatimSpan":"non-essential trips outside the AIIR are not permitted while smear-positive; a surgical mask is used on the patient only for medically necessary transport, and visitors follow the facility airborne-precautions policy with instruction and offering of an N95 respirator"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The Stage 2 item asks which reply is best, and Stage 3 supplies the scripted answer sentence containing all three keyed elements, simultaneously eliminating the surgical-mask-trip and surgical-mask-visitor distractors. Nothing about the Stage 2 decision requires the later resolution narrative. The Chinese Stage 3 text repeats the same three clauses, so the give-away is present in both languages."}
{"rowToken":"r_bf25638f12fc445d6da2548206a4","verdict":"NO_LEAK_COMPLETE_RECORD","testedDecision":"What the nurse must transmit to the rapid response team at 1100 given a charted Full Code and a capacitated refusal of intubation.","stageEvaluations":{"s_5c1db45c255571654d6852eab7d6":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_827caffcd754c7523a8479519ab8","e_715e194ec6e58167783929f3b31d"],"basis":"Stage 1 establishes the documented verbatim refusal, the capacity assessment, the unavailable attending, and Dr. Reeves's 1100 deferral, which are the capacity and escalation elements of the keyed handoff."},"s_89375969fff7a87ae17e18f6ea94":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_72d9e362f253a1fd3b25fb5f60dc","e_8d12b0f290c080081984e053c40f"],"basis":"The 0950 page and the 1030 gasped 'don't let them put a tube in me' extend the escalation history and confirm the refusal persists while the code status is still Full Code."},"s_9eb3f94e10b1b31b5b636861e3e9":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_6820fbcd116208f0f45d41a5bfae","e_597ccc931ecfa28945a96f712da9"],"basis":"Stage 3 supplies the 1100 RRT activation moment named in the stem and the arriving critical-care fellow who must receive the report."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_52da642f229bca2e0996022f1711","e_e2775fbefeaa02c1751b7dac1806","e_a383334e676ac0c21e3592f35b50","e_c001c8d6fd4ed4a7a77c481e8e5d"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_827caffcd754c7523a8479519ab8","e_715e194ec6e58167783929f3b31d","e_72d9e362f253a1fd3b25fb5f60dc","e_6820fbcd116208f0f45d41a5bfae","e_597ccc931ecfa28945a96f712da9"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The keyed option explicitly bundles 'escalation history,' which only exists because Stages 1 and 2 record the attending's unavailability and two deferred pages, so the item is built on the full record. Stage 3 narrates that Dr. Anand independently questioned the patient and entered DNAR/DNI, but it never states what the nurse's handoff contained, so it does not supply the keyed content. Both language versions describe the same escalation sequence."}
{"rowToken":"r_6c18491544b9ee1c4ed9ef57b5c0","verdict":"NO_LEAK_COMPLETE_RECORD","testedDecision":"Which of four documented breaches created the widest and most direct cross-contamination pathway to other clients on the unit.","stageEvaluations":{"s_3257930b504f943c60cad87473ab":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_a69e147066181ffc0ca25590ea1c","e_cae1ce2a5712f9eb4e31d71e74f9"],"basis":"Stage 1 supplies the competing door-handle option: the student touched the Room 12 handle with bare unwashed hands and targeted disinfection was arranged."},"s_826f9f674b09c3066fb100eedbe3":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_0e5c5594a677c991609fda799da3"],"basis":"Stage 2 supplies three of the four options, including the keyed one: Client C's stethoscope carried from Room 16 onto the clean-utility counter beside the clean supply cart, plus the linen bag and the wife at the doorway."},"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"basis":"Stage 3 carries the 1230 charge-nurse prompt that the stem reproduces and confirms all four breaches were identified for comparison."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_e4b0d424f878c57152ebe5954deb","e_b346040fbff750d3c5cae2f6e407","e_3d4735e8dd969be49de3d2d18ce0","e_a5f8f46640c01ed79eaf81b0d0a2"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_a69e147066181ffc0ca25590ea1c","e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_0e5c5594a677c991609fda799da3","e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"Ranking these four breaches by exposure radius is impossible without the Stage 1 door handle and the Stage 2 stethoscope, linen bag, and doorway visitor, so the item is deliberately built on the full shift record. The Stage 3 remediation of clean-utility surfaces restates the pathway already given in Stage 2 rather than adding a new discriminating cue, and no stage ranks the breaches. Chinese and English carry the same four incidents and the same remediation wording."}
{"rowToken":"r_e810c9c62065d4bfb17692ad3e89","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which discharge risks must be transmitted in the postoperative day 3 SBAR report to the receiving home health nurse.","stageEvaluations":{"s_d04a9853ff9f71f87b6e755a67d8":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_9daebc635df4e848161b64a1bfc4","e_ea6b5e59a1e298992fc890b1bd72"],"basis":"POD1 establishes the failed teach-back and interpreter need, the creatinine rise to 1.5 mg/dL on rivaroxaban, and living alone in a second-floor walk-up without stair assessment."},"s_d7df06b90d8692fca363dd07d8b3":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_28b7e82c39c7dd6f95f276693456","e_acef1afa08d550c7c034c146447c"],"basis":"POD2 supplies the rivaroxaban 10 mg for 35 days teaching, the creatinine return to 1.4 mg/dL after hydration, the equipment list, and the son's role as phone contact after the son's coverage ends."},"s_b68f88b760f79e5391dbc39661df":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_fd20273907ec6b92c364f9cb0f61","e_fb7491a652aeefa506f0af324175"],"basis":"The POD3 note itemizes exactly what the nurse communicated in the SBAR call, matching keyed options A, B, C, D, and F while omitting any hot-tea or oxycodone-refusal content, which is the distractor."},"":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":[],"basis":""}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_6f8928d0d50ff13a5fb207eade3b","e_144bf90b10aa145e7e833f686d12","e_b5a1d73f3c3c9d446b96bd9341fd","e_8dd90dee7aa0cf27a6ce758fb3fc"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_9daebc635df4e848161b64a1bfc4","e_28b7e82c39c7dd6f95f276693456","e_acef1afa08d550c7c034c146447c","e_fd20273907ec6b92c364f9cb0f61","e_fb7491a652aeefa506f0af324175"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_b68f88b760f79e5391dbc39661df","evidenceIds":["e_fd20273907ec6b92c364f9cb0f61","e_fb7491a652aeefa506f0af324175"],"verbatimSpan":"communicates language needs, the need for an interpreter for teaching, social situation, functional status, equipment, rivaroxaban dose/duration and adherence concerns, renal-function trend, bleeding surveillance, son's role as the communication contact, and the confirmed follow-up appointment"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"The select-all asks which items are essential in the SBAR, and the POD3 narrative already lists the handoff contents in a single sentence that maps one-to-one onto the five keyed options. That enumeration is not needed to reason about transition risk, which is fully supported by POD1 and POD2. The Chinese POD3 sentence lists the identical items in the same order."}
{"rowToken":"r_49ca241dcaa1b8b98b9f8318d062","verdict":"LEAK","testedDecision":"Which teaching actions for Client C's unoriented daughter correctly support MRSA contact precautions while keeping her safely involved in care.","stageEvaluations":{"s_3257930b504f943c60cad87473ab":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_931f2afc1daa0116bf8de24f68d4","e_8f04f847a046caaa836c371ccb8f"],"basis":"The 0800 finding that the daughter is repositioning her father in street clothes and says nobody told her to wear anything creates the teaching need and supports the nonjudgmental acknowledgment option."},"s_826f9f674b09c3066fb100eedbe3":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83"],"basis":"Stage 2's Client C content is the 0945 stethoscope found in the clean utility room during the dressing change, which concerns staff equipment handling rather than visitor PPE teaching."},"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5":{"contribution":"UNSAFE","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"basis":"The end-of-shift line that the daughter correctly demonstrates gown and glove donning and doffing confirms the keyed PPE pair and the return-demonstration option while implicitly excluding the surgical-mask and no-touching distractors."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_5d1761ae49822d9cca0cd059f163","e_9b2ddf4db6bb984c117265ca60fd","e_ca34495f6b2b057be169fabfd1cf","e_e037720a52b74bdf50060b925778"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_931f2afc1daa0116bf8de24f68d4","e_8f04f847a046caaa836c371ccb8f","e_13dcaa529a7705927039e8a0de83","e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"locus":{"stageToken":"s_7e4fe7ac245010b30f563b53f0e5","evidenceIds":["e_0f3fd6932a548249dd0eefbc2cd6","e_beca8a82edd61c3ab73a4cf4e52b"],"verbatimSpan":"Client C's daughter correctly demonstrates gown and glove donning and doffing"},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"This Stage 1 select-all turns on choosing gown-and-glove teaching plus return demonstration over a surgical mask or a blanket no-touching restriction, and the Stage 3 outcome states that the daughter successfully performed gown and glove donning and doffing. That outcome is unnecessary for a Stage 1 teaching decision yet confirms two keyed options and shows she remained involved in care. The Chinese end-of-shift sentence states the same successful return demonstration."}
{"rowToken":"r_9772ec790f8025c87bc017af2fbc","verdict":"NO_LEAK_NONANSWERING_DATA","testedDecision":"How the nurse should counsel the couple about leafy greens and vitamin K intake as warfarin therapy is being established.","stageEvaluations":{"s_be106b518d818f7a1e8304bf7f46":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_59d9fb01bdb4b938f93e5762eca7"],"basis":"Stage 1's frozen spinach and kale purchase to 'build his blood back up' is background misconception color; it neither states the consistency principle nor rules out the elimination plan."},"s_1dbac0db77eadb43613d5c9de5d5":{"contribution":"NONANSWERING","evidenceIds":["e_957cb81ac3a6aab98f9207765711"],"basis":"Stage 2 concerns the ibuprofen discontinuation, acetaminophen order, and enoxaparin injection return demonstration, none of which bears on vitamin K counseling."},"s_e77fd303477ade86df938a248001":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_17133dd71f7b7a288ef2f41da8d9","e_6a0fdc9393ff91c85fe4d09fc8b7"],"basis":"Stage 3 presents the patient's question about eating many green vegetables and the wife's printed 'foods to avoid on warfarin' list she intends to cut out entirely."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_c906e4202c90c34991237ab85387","e_f5e72d28a2c2cdaee1985da9a60f","e_d6b8ec6949b1a427d4a3d93c5606","e_6dface517e4a2a785ceb1d3a7aca"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_59d9fb01bdb4b938f93e5762eca7","e_957cb81ac3a6aab98f9207765711","e_17133dd71f7b7a288ef2f41da8d9","e_6a0fdc9393ff91c85fe4d09fc8b7"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"No visible stage ever states that vitamin K intake should be kept consistent, so the keyed teaching must still be supplied by the learner. The extra Stage 1 spinach purchase and Stage 2 injection teaching sit beside the item without resolving between eliminating, increasing, or stabilizing greens. Both language versions present the wife's avoidance list without correcting it."}
{"rowToken":"r_bf1a21353f85d9d31f855583bd26","verdict":"NO_LEAK_COMPLETE_RECORD","testedDecision":"Which follow-through actions after the 1130 DNAR/DNI entry evaluate the advocacy process, and which documentation timing does not.","stageEvaluations":{"s_5c1db45c255571654d6852eab7d6":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_827caffcd754c7523a8479519ab8","e_715e194ec6e58167783929f3b31d"],"basis":"The 0745 unavailable attending and Dr. Reeves's 0800 promise to come around 1100 create the delayed-provider-response gap that the incident-report option asks the learner to recognize."},"s_89375969fff7a87ae17e18f6ea94":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_72d9e362f253a1fd3b25fb5f60dc","e_8d12b0f290c080081984e053c40f"],"basis":"The 0950 page answered only with 'as soon as I can' while the patient desaturated documents the second deferral that makes the system-vulnerability report a defensible evaluation action."},"s_9eb3f94e10b1b31b5b636861e3e9":{"contribution":"REQUIRED","evidenceIds":["e_6820fbcd116208f0f45d41a5bfae","e_597ccc931ecfa28945a96f712da9"],"basis":"Stage 3 supplies the 1130 reference point: Dr. Anand's DNAR/DNI order, the daughter completing the advance directive, and the palliative consult that the verification actions must close out."}},"partEvidenceIds":["e_c75e0898c2ddaed9297b60c4e09e","e_a7983b5ff0f501e609348d8b7872","e_c8373fcc8ca7bbf883bc5d29f875","e_4db17e8a6d3b3b5cf7c1805e241c"],"stageEvidenceIds":["e_827caffcd754c7523a8479519ab8","e_72d9e362f253a1fd3b25fb5f60dc","e_8d12b0f290c080081984e053c40f","e_6820fbcd116208f0f45d41a5bfae","e_597ccc931ecfa28945a96f712da9"],"locus":{"stageToken":null,"evidenceIds":[],"verbatimSpan":null},"bilingualRelation":"PARALLEL","reason":"Option D turns on the two documented provider delays in Stages 1 and 2, so the earlier stages are working parts of this evaluation item rather than surplus. Stage 3 records that the order was entered and the family was engaged but never states that the EHR entry, wristband check, or incident report should be verified, and it never addresses retrospective versus contemporaneous charting. The Chinese stages record the same deferrals and the same 1130 resolution."}
